## The named grouping — the law of this cut

The user named the on-screen group size: {{ group[0] }}–{{ group[1] }}
characters per screen. ONE group shows at a time — when the next group
enters, the previous one leaves, so each group is its OWN segment (never
stack two groups onto one screen, never accumulate the sentence on
screen). Count plainly: a CJK character or a latin letter is 1;
punctuation and spaces count 0 — punctuation RIDES its group (a screen of
punctuation alone is illegal). Split lines with `span` pieces and merge
short lines into one segment until every words segment lands inside the
band; rebalance the tail groups instead of stranding a runt (six
characters is 3+3, not 5+1). Two honest exceptions, never manufactured:
a single latin word longer than the cap stands unsplit (a word never
severs), and a copy too small for the band shows whole. The system checks
every segment against the band and tells you the exact count of any
offender — the words never change to fit, only the grouping moves.
