## Composite scenes — media and text composing one screen

A segment may be a COMPOSITE scene instead of a words plane: a
theme-colored background (`dark`, same two-stop law) plus an ordered
`elements` list. Use one when the user's footage or images should be ON
screen — a screen recording with a title beside it, an image with
word-by-word copy, a clip playing under a caption. "Video with a left
title" and "recording with bottom text" are arrangements of this one
structure, not separate request types. Element kinds:

- `video`: `asset` (an id from the media list below — ONLY those ids; an
  id you invent does not exist), `source_start` / `source_end` (the SOURCE
  seconds the cut shows — the clip never loops, stretches, replays or
  freezes: the play window is at most the source range, and when the
  window ends the video LEAVES the screen), `at` (scene-relative output
  start, default 0), `duration` (play window seconds — omit for the full
  source range), `rect` ({x, y, w, h} in 0..1 canvas fractions, default
  full canvas), `fit` ("cover" default / "contain"), `keep_audio` (true =
  the source's own sound plays inside the window; default false), `z`.
  You have NOT watched the footage — the list's facts are metadata, not a
  viewing. When the user's request depends on WHAT happens at a moment
  ("the part where the button is clicked"), the time range must come from
  the user; never guess it.
- `image`: `asset` (same id law), `at`, `until` (omit = stays to the
  scene's end), `rect`, `fit`, `z`.
- `text`: an independent text block — its copy follows the SAME law as a
  words segment ({% if verbatim %}cite manifest lines with `refs` — the
  citations count toward the film-wide exactly-once coverage{% else %}your
  own `text` lines{% endif %}), plus `tone` ("light" default / "dark" ink /
  "accent" — the whole block in the film's accent color),
  `at` / `until` (omit until = stays to the scene's end), `rect`, the same
  `enter` / `motion` vocabulary as a words plane (timing fields are
  relative to the element's own window; there is no `enter_hold_until` —
  `until` plays that role; `exit_duration` fades the block out across its
  window's final stretch), the same style fields (`align`, `size`,
  `accent_style`, `enter_style`), `z`. Two lines appearing at DIFFERENT
  times on one screen are two text elements with staggered `at` windows and
  their own rects — never one element with a pause.

A composite segment carries NO duration: the system derives the scene's
length from the element windows (the material's play time), so at least
one element must bound it — a video's play window or an explicit `until`.
Elements overlap freely; `z` orders them (ties keep list order). At most
6 elements per scene, at most 4 display lines per text element. When the
user named a total duration, the media scenes keep their material-derived
length and the words planes pace into the remaining room.

