## The vocabulary (closed enums)

- `motion`: "static" (default), "push" (the text plane slowly settles in),
  "pull" (slowly eases out). Use a move where the words ask for emphasis;
  most planes stay static.
- `enter`: "lines" (default — each display line enters with a stagger),
  "words" (the words appear one by one — for keyword beats the user names).
- Precise timing fields (seconds relative to the segment's start; omit them
  unless the user explicitly names numbers): `enter_start` (first token),
  `enter_interval` (between adjacent token starts), `enter_duration` (one
  token's entrance), and `enter_hold_until` (the hold endpoint — the words
  leave at that moment; omit it for "stay until the scene ends"). They are legal only with `enter="words"`.
  `exit_duration` fades the words OUT across the window's final stretch
  (ending at the hold endpoint) — the background never fades; without it
  the words simply stop at the endpoint. `motion_start` / `motion_end` bound
  a `push` or `pull` move and `motion_scale` tunes its amplitude (default
  1.06; a subtle continued settle after the words land ≈ 1.02–1.04); omit
  both bounds for the full scene. The system converts seconds to the
  nearest frame and rejects an arrangement that does not fit the segment —
  never shorten or drop the user's words to make it fit.
- Style fields (omit for the default look): `align` "center" (default left),
  `size` "small" / "large" (medium is the signature size), `accent_style`
  "color" — the cited `*emphasis*` spans render in the film's accent color
  instead of the inverted tile. A larger size fits FEWER characters per
  line — the system rejects an overflow with the exact count.
  `enter_style` "fade" — the entrance EFFECT, orthogonal to `enter`'s
  organization (lines/words = WHO arrives together): the default "rise"
  fades + rises + blurs in; "fade" changes opacity only — no rise, no blur.
  A group arriving TOGETHER with a pure quick fade-in is `enter="words"` +
  `enter_interval=0` (simultaneous) + a short `enter_duration` (quick) +
  `enter_style="fade"` — interval 0 alone makes the arrival simultaneous
  but does NOT remove the rise/blur.
- `dark`: true/false to pin a plane's background, null to let the system
  alternate.
- `seconds`: optional words-scene pacing proposal, 1–120 seconds. The system
  quantizes to half-second steps and rescales to a named total; composite
  scenes still derive duration from their element windows. Animation must
  fit the resulting window, and the complete film must fit the render cap.

When the user asks for the punchy typography look (short word-groups
replacing each other, text arriving and leaving quickly, a colored keyword,
two lines co-dwelling), the vocabulary composes so: ONE group per segment
(the grouping law above), a fast group entrance (`enter="words"` with a
small `enter_interval`/`enter_duration`; `enter_interval=0` arrives the
whole group at once; `enter_style="fade"` when the words should NOT rise
or blur on arrival), `exit_duration` for the group to
fade out as it leaves, a subtle `push` at `motion_scale` ≈ 1.02–1.04 for
the continued settle after landing, `align="center"` with a `size` tier for
the centered poster look, and `accent_style="color"` (or a composite text
element's `tone="accent"`) for the colored keyword. Two lines at different
moments on one screen = a composite scene with two text elements at
staggered `at` windows.

